#!/usr/bin/python3
"""sfduo-fold: the fold effect - the picture rises out of the depth as the device opens.

    sfduo-fold                run (with the session; needs sfduo-posture)
    sfduo-fold --demo A       hold the effect at hinge angle A, for a look
    sfduo-fold --simulate S   drive it with a scripted hinge: "150:0,0:4,150:4"
                              (angle:seconds, ...) - hold, then move over the seconds
    sfduo-fold --bench        one sweep with the overlay up, frame times reported

Below the half-open book the picture is not the screen but a sheet: hinged at
the hinge, turned away from the glass, seen in perspective, blurred and dimmed
towards its outer edge. Opening the device brings the sheet forward until, at
the book angle, it lies flat and sharp and the overlay goes; folding shut
takes it back into the depth. The idea is Mac Duo's (a MacBook lid) and the
iPhone Duo's (a cover screen opening into the inner one), done with the Duo's
own hinge on its own two panels (#36).

How: the screen is captured once (grim) and kept as a GL texture with a mip
pyramid; a GTK4 layer surface over everything, input passing through, holds a
GLArea that draws each half as the quad its sheet is, the rasteriser doing
the perspective and one fragment shader the rest - a mip level with a
five-tap kernel for the blur, and the dimming. The angle comes smoothed from sfduo-posture (#55) and is led a little
to cover the sensor's lag. GL through ctypes: a clean image has no PyOpenGL.

Every number is an environment variable (FOLD_*, see `numbers` below): the
effect is tuned by eye on the device, not by editing this file.
"""
